1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,267
This is ECE 358, video 4 a. The topics are protocol layers and encapsulation, with question bank questions 44 to 51.

2
00:00:12,717 --> 00:00:18,669
After this video, you can name the 5 Internet layers, with their jobs and protocols.

3
00:00:19,119 --> 00:00:24,260
You can explain encapsulation: message, segment, datagram and frame.

4
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:27,889
You can calculate the header overhead of a frame.

5
00:00:28,339 --> 00:00:31,965
And you can answer questions 44 to 51.

6
00:00:32,415 --> 00:00:38,730
Video 4 b then solves the long question bank problem about a path of four routers.

7
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:44,257
Chapter 1 showed many parts of a network. The picture shows a small network.

8
00:00:44,707 --> 00:00:50,553
The slide lists the pieces of a network. First, hosts. Here are three hosts and a server.

9
00:00:51,003 --> 00:00:53,157
Routers. Here are four routers.

10
00:00:53,607 --> 00:00:57,981
Links of various media. The links connect the hosts and the routers.

11
00:00:58,431 --> 00:01:05,449
Applications, protocols, and hardware and software. A real network has millions of these pieces.

12
00:01:05,899 --> 00:01:14,710
So the slide asks a question. How can we organize the structure of the network? And how can we organize our discussion of networks?

13
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:20,664
The answer is layers. The slides explain layers with an example from air travel.

14
00:01:21,114 --> 00:01:27,706
Think of a trip by airplane. At the departure airport, you do a series of steps, from top to bottom.

15
00:01:28,156 --> 00:01:30,055
First, you purchase a ticket.

16
00:01:30,505 --> 00:01:31,955
You check your baggage.

17
00:01:32,405 --> 00:01:35,392
You go through the gate, and the airplane is loaded.

18
00:01:35,842 --> 00:01:38,082
The airplane takes off from the runway.

19
00:01:38,532 --> 00:01:40,516
Then airplane routing starts.

20
00:01:40,966 --> 00:01:44,486
Between the two airports, the airplane follows its route.

21
00:01:44,936 --> 00:01:54,067
At the arrival airport, the same kinds of steps occur in the opposite order, from the bottom up: from the runway landing, back to the ticket.

22
00:01:54,517 --> 00:02:04,117
The full system gives one end-to-end service. It moves a person plus baggage. It is a series of steps, and it involves many services.

23
00:02:04,567 --> 00:02:11,137
Now put the matching steps in one row. Ticket purchase and ticket complaint form the ticketing service.

24
00:02:11,587 --> 00:02:15,129
Baggage check and baggage claim form the baggage service.

25
00:02:15,579 --> 00:02:19,056
Gates load and gates unload form the gate service.

26
00:02:19,506 --> 00:02:23,367
Runway takeoff and runway landing form the runway service.

27
00:02:23,817 --> 00:02:29,428
Airplane routing at both ends forms the routing service. Each row is one layer.

28
00:02:29,878 --> 00:02:42,614
Look at the baggage layer. It has its own internal actions. For example, the airline puts a tag on the bag, and reads the tag at arrival. The passenger does not see these actions.

29
00:02:43,064 --> 00:02:50,275
The baggage layer relies on the layer below. The gate service loads the bag into the airplane, and unloads it.

30
00:02:50,725 --> 00:02:55,503
The baggage layer does not need to know how the runway or the routing work.

31
00:02:55,953 --> 00:03:07,495
This gives the definition of a layer. Each layer implements a service. It uses its own internal-layer actions. And it relies on the services of the layer below.

32
00:03:07,945 --> 00:03:16,926
Why do we use layers? First, layering gives an explicit structure. It identifies the pieces of the system and their relationships.

33
00:03:17,376 --> 00:03:24,907
Each layer gives a service to the layer above. The layered reference model is also a common base for discussion.

34
00:03:25,357 --> 00:03:42,637
Second, layering is modularization. It supports 3 key attributes of large distributed systems. The first attribute is seamless evolution. For example, IPv6 can replace IPv4 in the network layer.

35
00:03:43,087 --> 00:03:44,985
The other layers do not change.

36
00:03:45,435 --> 00:03:54,865
The second attribute is ease of scalability. For example, a new tier 1 network can join the Internet. It uses the same layers.

37
00:03:55,315 --> 00:04:06,472
The third attribute is easy maintenance and updating. For example, you can remove old links and switches and add new ones. This changes the physical layer.

38
00:04:06,922 --> 00:04:09,397
But the transport layer needs no change.

39
00:04:09,847 --> 00:04:20,343
The rule: a change inside one layer is transparent to the rest of the system. This is true when the service of that layer to the layer above stays the same.

40
00:04:20,793 --> 00:04:26,894
The Internet has 5 layers. The picture shows host A, a router R, and host B.

41
00:04:27,344 --> 00:04:38,139
The top layer is the application layer. It supports network applications. Examples are HTTP, I MAP, S M T P and DNS.

42
00:04:38,589 --> 00:04:42,066
The application on A talks to the application on B.

43
00:04:42,516 --> 00:04:51,732
Below it is the transport layer. It transfers data from one process to another process. Examples are TCP and UDP.

44
00:04:52,182 --> 00:04:57,195
Its reach is process to process, from a program on A to a program on B.

45
00:04:57,645 --> 00:05:10,360
Next is the network layer. It routes datagrams, also called IP packets, from the source machine to the destination machine. Examples are IP and the routing protocols.

46
00:05:10,810 --> 00:05:13,775
Its reach is host to host, through the routers.

47
00:05:14,225 --> 00:05:27,239
Next is the link layer. It transfers data between neighboring network elements, such as hosts and switches. Examples are Ethernet, 802.11 WiFi, and PPP.

48
00:05:27,689 --> 00:05:29,779
Its reach is one link only.

49
00:05:30,229 --> 00:05:34,539
The bottom layer is the physical layer. It puts the bits on the wire.

50
00:05:34,989 --> 00:05:39,639
From bottom to top, each layer extends the reach of the layer below it.

51
00:05:40,089 --> 00:05:53,529
Now follow data down the layers of the source host. The application layer exchanges messages. We call the message M. To send it, the application uses the service of the transport layer.

52
00:05:53,979 --> 00:06:02,726
The transport layer encapsulates M. It puts the transport-layer header, H t, in front of M. The result is a segment.

53
00:06:03,176 --> 00:06:19,667
H t carries the control information of the transport layer. Examples are the sequence number, the ACK, and an error checking code. The transport protocol uses H t to give its service, for example reliable data transfer.

54
00:06:20,117 --> 00:06:31,274
The network layer encapsulates the segment. It puts the network-layer header, H n, in front. The result is a datagram, also called an IP packet.

55
00:06:31,724 --> 00:06:40,983
H n carries the source IP address, the destination IP address, an error checking code, and the time to live, TTL.

56
00:06:41,433 --> 00:06:49,667
The link layer encapsulates the datagram. It puts the link-layer header, H l, in front. The result is a frame.

57
00:06:50,117 --> 00:06:59,227
H l carries the control information of the link layer. The link layer moves the frame to the neighboring device, with the physical layer.

58
00:06:59,677 --> 00:07:14,333
This is the principle of encapsulation. Each layer takes the unit from the layer above. It puts its own header in front. Then it gives the result to the layer below. The layer does not change the unit from above.

59
00:07:14,783 --> 00:07:21,268
Here is the complete picture. At the source, the data goes down the stack. Each layer adds a header.

60
00:07:21,718 --> 00:07:25,835
The names of the units are message, segment, datagram, and frame.

61
00:07:26,285 --> 00:07:30,104
The physical layer sends the bits of the frame over the medium.

62
00:07:30,554 --> 00:07:43,269
At the destination, the data goes up the stack. Each layer reads its own header and removes it. Then it gives the rest to the layer above. The application gets the original message M.

63
00:07:43,719 --> 00:07:47,111
Take a message of 100 bytes as an example.

64
00:07:47,561 --> 00:07:58,931
Use the header sizes of slide 63. The TCP header is 20 bytes. So the segment is 100 plus 20, which is 120 bytes.

65
00:07:59,381 --> 00:08:07,595
The IP header is 20 bytes. So the datagram is 120 plus 20, which is 140 bytes.

66
00:08:08,045 --> 00:08:16,642
The Ethernet header is 14 bytes. So the frame is 140 plus 14, which is 154 bytes.

67
00:08:17,092 --> 00:08:25,924
At the destination, the sizes go back down in the same column: 140, 120, and 100 bytes.

68
00:08:26,374 --> 00:08:39,494
Each header is for the same layer on the other side. The headers go on in the order transport, network, link. So the destination removes them in the opposite order: link, network, transport.

69
00:08:39,944 --> 00:08:48,136
Slide 62 adds the devices between the two hosts. The source host makes the frame. It has all 5 layers.

70
00:08:48,586 --> 00:08:54,922
First the frame reaches a link-layer switch. A switch has only two layers: link and physical.

71
00:08:55,372 --> 00:09:01,025
The switch reads only the link header, H l. Then it sends the frame on the next link.

72
00:09:01,475 --> 00:09:07,385
The switch is transparent to the source host and to the router. They do not know that it is there.

73
00:09:07,835 --> 00:09:13,979
Next the frame reaches a router. A router has three layers: network, link, and physical.

74
00:09:14,429 --> 00:09:22,621
The router removes H l and reads H n, for example the destination IP address. It selects the next link.

75
00:09:23,071 --> 00:09:27,849
Then it puts a new H l in front of the datagram, and sends the frame.

76
00:09:28,299 --> 00:09:36,491
At the destination host, the data goes up all 5 layers. Each layer removes its header. The application gets M.

77
00:09:36,941 --> 00:09:39,032
So a host uses 5 layers.

78
00:09:39,482 --> 00:09:43,215
A router uses 3 layers and reads the network header.

79
00:09:43,665 --> 00:09:50,364
A switch uses 2 layers and reads the link header. No router or switch reads H t or M.

80
00:09:50,814 --> 00:10:05,022
Slide 63 gives the advantages of encapsulation. First, the layers are modular. Each layer touches only its own header. So a change in one layer does not need a change in another layer.

81
00:10:05,472 --> 00:10:17,845
Second, encapsulation protects the data of the upper layers. A layer can put an error checking code in its header. The receiving layer uses it to find bit errors in the data from above.

82
00:10:18,295 --> 00:10:25,890
The disadvantage is overhead. Every header must also be transmitted, but it does not carry application data.

83
00:10:26,340 --> 00:10:37,817
The slide gives the header lengths. The TCP header is 20 bytes or more. The IP header is 20 bytes or more. The Ethernet header is 14 bytes or more.

84
00:10:38,267 --> 00:10:45,542
Draw the 154-byte frame of our example to scale. The message uses 100 bytes.

85
00:10:45,992 --> 00:10:51,347
The three headers use 14 plus 20 plus 20, which is 54 bytes.

86
00:10:51,797 --> 00:11:06,303
The overhead fraction is the header bytes divided by the frame bytes. Here, 54 divided by 154 is 35.1 percent. So more than one third of this frame is overhead.

87
00:11:06,753 --> 00:11:17,271
Here is the method to calculate the encapsulation overhead. Add the header lengths: 20 plus 20 plus 14 is 54 bytes per frame.

88
00:11:17,721 --> 00:11:22,670
Then divide by the frame length, which is the message length plus 54.

89
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:29,435
The orange curve shows this fraction for message sizes up to 1500 bytes.

90
00:11:29,885 --> 00:11:37,927
A 100-byte message gives 54 divided by 154. That is 35.1 percent.

91
00:11:38,377 --> 00:11:58,324
A 946-byte message fills a 1000-byte frame: 946 plus 54 is 1000. Its overhead is 54 divided by 1000, which is only 5.4 percent. We meet this frame again in the long question of video 4 b.

92
00:11:58,774 --> 00:12:11,041
The header cost is fixed for each frame. Small messages pay the same 54 bytes. So the overhead fraction is high for small messages, and low for large messages.

93
00:12:11,491 --> 00:12:25,784
Now the question bank. Questions 44 and 47 ask how many layers the Internet model has, and for one example protocol in each layer, from link to application. Pause the video and try it.

94
00:12:26,234 --> 00:12:34,255
Count the layers on slide 57. Application, transport, network, link, and physical. There are 5 layers.

95
00:12:34,705 --> 00:12:42,428
Application layer: HTTP. Other examples are I MAP, S M T P and DNS.

96
00:12:42,878 --> 00:12:47,443
Transport layer: TCP. Another example is UDP.

97
00:12:47,893 --> 00:12:52,907
Network layer: IP. The routing protocols are also in this layer.

98
00:12:53,357 --> 00:12:59,949
Link layer: Ethernet. Other examples are 802.11 WiFi and PPP.

99
00:13:00,399 --> 00:13:03,087
The physical layer puts the bits on the wire.

100
00:13:03,537 --> 00:13:11,814
For the answer, one protocol per layer is enough. For example HTTP, TCP, IP, and Ethernet.

101
00:13:12,264 --> 00:13:19,197
Question 45. Why is the Internet software organized as layers? Pause the video and try it.

102
00:13:19,647 --> 00:13:32,682
Use slide 56. First reason: layering gives an explicit structure. It identifies the pieces of the system and their relationships. It also gives a reference model for discussion.

103
00:13:33,132 --> 00:13:36,417
Second reason: layering is modularization.

104
00:13:36,867 --> 00:13:42,734
It supports seamless evolution, such as IPv4 to IPv6.

105
00:13:43,184 --> 00:13:47,621
It supports ease of scalability, such as a new tier 1 network.

106
00:13:48,071 --> 00:13:52,231
And it supports easy maintenance, such as new links and switches.

107
00:13:52,681 --> 00:14:04,948
So a good answer says this. Layering gives an explicit structure, and it supports evolution, scalability, and maintenance. A change in one layer is transparent to the other layers.

108
00:14:05,398 --> 00:14:11,051
Question 46. Explain the function of each layer. Pause the video and try it.

109
00:14:11,501 --> 00:14:19,053
Use slide 57. Application: it supports network applications, for example HTTP.

110
00:14:19,503 --> 00:14:26,223
Transport: it transfers data from one process to another process, for example TCP.

111
00:14:26,673 --> 00:14:33,884
Network: it routes datagrams from the source machine to the destination machine. The protocol is IP.

112
00:14:34,334 --> 00:14:40,030
Link: it transfers data between neighboring network elements, for example Ethernet.

113
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:42,955
Physical: it puts the bits on the wire.

114
00:14:43,405 --> 00:14:52,301
Questions 48 and 49. Explain the principle of encapsulation, and give its advantages. Pause the video and try it.

115
00:14:52,751 --> 00:14:56,633
The principle: each layer takes the unit from the layer above.

116
00:14:57,083 --> 00:14:59,217
It puts its own header in front.

117
00:14:59,667 --> 00:15:08,648
Then it gives the result to the layer below. The picture shows the network layer: it puts H n in front of the segment and makes a datagram.

118
00:15:09,098 --> 00:15:18,570
The header carries the control information of that layer. The same layer at the receiver reads the header, removes it, and passes the rest up.

119
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:28,599
The advantages are on slide 63. First, the layers are modular, so a change in one layer needs no change in another layer.

120
00:15:29,049 --> 00:15:34,446
Second, the layer-specific error checking codes protect the data of the upper layers.

121
00:15:34,896 --> 00:15:45,989
Questions 50 and 51. How is the encapsulation overhead calculated? And what are the key words for the unit of each layer? Pause the video and try it.

122
00:15:46,439 --> 00:15:56,423
For question 50, add the header lengths of all layers. With the minimum sizes, that is 20 plus 20 plus 14, which is 54 bytes.

123
00:15:56,873 --> 00:16:01,417
The overhead fraction is the header bytes divided by the frame length.

124
00:16:01,867 --> 00:16:07,542
If the frame length is fixed, the message bytes are the frame length minus the header bytes.

125
00:16:07,992 --> 00:16:14,733
For question 51, use slide 61. The application layer exchanges messages.

126
00:16:15,183 --> 00:16:17,786
The transport layer exchanges segments.

127
00:16:18,236 --> 00:16:23,015
The network layer exchanges datagrams, also called IP packets.

128
00:16:23,465 --> 00:16:25,939
And the link layer exchanges frames.

129
00:16:26,389 --> 00:16:30,656
Each unit is the unit of the layer above, plus one more header.

130
00:16:31,106 --> 00:16:40,514
Here is the recap. One. A layer implements a service with its own internal actions, and relies on the service of the layer below.

131
00:16:40,964 --> 00:16:51,652
Two. Layering gives an explicit structure. It supports evolution, scalability, and maintenance. A change in one layer is transparent to the other layers.

132
00:16:52,102 --> 00:17:01,681
Three. The Internet has 5 layers. Application, transport, network, link, and physical. Know one protocol and the reach of each layer.

133
00:17:02,131 --> 00:17:13,821
Four. In encapsulation, each layer adds its own header. The units are message, segment, datagram, and frame. The receiver removes the headers in the opposite order.

134
00:17:14,271 --> 00:17:20,053
Five. A host uses 5 layers. A router uses 3, and a switch uses 2.

135
00:17:20,503 --> 00:17:35,436
Six. The overhead is the sum of the headers, 54 bytes with the minimum sizes. Divide by the frame length to get the overhead fraction. The next video, 4 b, solves the long Chapter 1 question.
